# RaceDay: Event Management for South African Road Running, Walking & Cycling Events

RaceDay is a web-based event management system for the South African road running, walking and cycling community. **Organisers** create and manage events, categories and results. **Participants** browse upcoming events, enter by choosing a category, track their enrolments and see their personal race history.

This is an individual Portfolio of Evidence built in three parts:

| Part | What was delivered |
|---|---|
| 1. Planning | ERD, API endpoint plan and SQL script (in `/docs`) |
| 2. API | ASP.NET Core 8 Web API, EF Core Code-First, JWT authentication with roles, Swagger, unit tests, CI |
| 3. Web app | ASP.NET Core MVC front end, Azure Blob Storage (through the API), Docker |

---

## Architecture

```
Browser  ->  RaceDay.Web (MVC)  ->  RaceDay.Api  ->  SQL Server
                                        |
                                        +---------->  Azure Blob Storage (images)
```

- The **MVC app never touches the database or Azure.** Every data operation is an HTTP call to the API, made by one class (`ApiClient`).
- **Login:** the MVC app sends credentials to the API, receives a JWT, and keeps the token, role, name and user ID in the ASP.NET Core **session**. The token is attached to every API call.
- **Role protection:** a `[SessionAuthorize]` filter protects every MVC controller by login and role, and the API enforces the roles again with `[Authorize(Roles = ...)]`.
- **Images:** the MVC app forwards uploads to the API, which stores them in Azure Blob Storage and saves only the image URL in the database.

